---
import MainLayout from "../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
import BeforeAfterSlider from "../components/ui/BeforeAfterSlider";
---

<MainLayout
    title="Utility LUTs"
    description="How the altn utility layer works: Apple Log and D-Log to Rec.709 conversion LUTs."
>
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-10 w-full">
        <div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-1 text-center">
            <h1 class="!mb-1">utility layer</h1>
            <div class="text-sayso-text/50 font-medium">
                the conversion step before the creative grade
            </div>
        </div>

        <section>
            <h2 class="!mb-2">how it works</h2>
            <p>
                Your footage is shot in log - flat, desaturated, designed to
                capture maximum dynamic range. Before any creative LUT can
                work, that log signal needs to be converted to a standard
                color space (Rec.709).
            </p>
            <p>
                That's what the utility LUTs do. They sit as the first node in
                your DaVinci Resolve node tree, converting your camera's log
                format to Rec.709. Every creative LUT in the pack assumes
                Rec.709 input - this is what keeps the set at 23 files instead
                of 46.
            </p>

            <div class="rounded-xl border border-sayso-text/10 bg-sayso-yellow/5 p-5 my-6 font-mono text-sm text-center">
                <span class="text-sayso-text/40">[Log footage]</span>
                <span class="text-sayso-yellow mx-3">&rarr;</span>
                <span class="text-sayso-yellow font-bold">Utility LUT</span>
                <span class="text-sayso-yellow mx-3">&rarr;</span>
                <span class="text-sayso-text/40">[Rec.709]</span>
                <span class="text-sayso-yellow mx-3">&rarr;</span>
                <span class="text-sayso-text font-bold">Creative LUT</span>
                <span class="text-sayso-yellow mx-3">&rarr;</span>
                <span class="text-sayso-text/40">[Final]</span>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
            <div class="lut-card">
                <div class="mb-4 w-full">
                    <BeforeAfterSlider
                        client:load
                        beforeImage="/previews/log_base.jpg"
                        afterImage="/previews/Altn_util1.jpg"
                        beforeAlt="D-Log"
                        afterAlt="Rec.709"
                    />
                </div>
                <div class="text-sayso-yellow font-bold text-lg mb-2">Altn_util1</div>
                <div class="text-sm text-sayso-text/50 mb-3">D-Log → Rec.709 · 33-point</div>
                <p class="!m-0 text-sm text-sayso-text/70 leading-relaxed">
                    For DJI drone footage (Phantom/X5/X7 family). Reused from
                    DJI's official X7 DLOG2Rec709 conversion - the neutral one,
                    not the stylized sRGB variant. 33-point grid matches DJI's
                    own reference precision.
                </p>
                <a
                    href="/luts/utility/Altn_util1.cube"
                    download="Altn_util1.cube"
                    class="inline-block mt-4 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-lg border border-sayso-yellow/20 text-sayso-yellow text-xs font-bold !no-underline hover:bg-sayso-yellow/10 transition-colors"
                >
                    download
                </a>
            </div>
            <div class="lut-card">
                <div class="mb-4 w-full">
                    <BeforeAfterSlider
                        client:load
                        beforeImage="/previews/log_base.jpg"
                        afterImage="/previews/Altn_util2.jpg"
                        beforeAlt="Apple Log"
                        afterAlt="Rec.709"
                    />
                </div>
                <div class="text-sayso-yellow font-bold text-lg mb-2">Altn_util2</div>
                <div class="text-sm text-sayso-text/50 mb-3">Apple Log → Rec.709 · 65-point</div>
                <p class="!m-0 text-sm text-sayso-text/70 leading-relaxed">
                    For iPhone ProRes 422 HQ footage shot in Apple Log. Built
                    from Apple's published transfer function: Apple Log inverse
                    OETF, BT.2020-to-BT.709 gamut matrix, Gamma 2.4 encode. No
                    tone mapping, no gamut mapping - pure mathematical conversion.
                </p>
                <a
                    href="/luts/utility/Altn_util2.cube"
                    download="Altn_util2.cube"
                    class="inline-block mt-4 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-lg border border-sayso-yellow/20 text-sayso-yellow text-xs font-bold !no-underline hover:bg-sayso-yellow/10 transition-colors"
                >
                    download
                </a>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2 class="!mb-2">DaVinci Resolve setup</h2>
            <ol class="text-sm space-y-2">
                <li>
                    <strong>Node 1 - Utility:</strong> right-click your clip in
                    the Media Pool, open Clip Attributes, and set the Input LUT
                    to <code>Altn_util1.cube</code> (D-Log) or
                    <code>Altn_util2.cube</code> (Apple Log). Alternatively, add
                    a Corrector node and apply the LUT there.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Node 2 - Creative:</strong> add a second Corrector
                    node after the utility node. Apply any creative LUT from the
                    pack here. This node receives Rec.709 input.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Never bake them together.</strong> Keep utility and
                    creative as separate nodes. This lets you swap creative LUTs
                    without touching the conversion, and swap cameras without
                    touching the grade.
                </li>
            </ol>
        </section>
    </div>
</MainLayout>
